// meteor-cfd case - SPEC-LIT S32's redesigned gate, mesh (b): rebuilt per
// SPEC-LIT S34 as a genuine 2-D PLANE channel, the resolved twin of
// `channelPeriodicFluxWF.jsonc` - streamwise-cyclic in x, `empty`
// front/back in z (S34.1), hot walls top and bottom in y, wall-normal mesh
// GRADED two-sided toward both hot walls (`mesh.grading`, 50 cells,
// `expansion: 200`) so the first cell sits near y+ ~ 1 - `lowRe`, a
// resolved sublayer. Identical cross-section, streamwise length, momentum
// source, q_w and compensating sink to the WF twin above; the ONLY
// physical difference is the mesh and the `lowRe` wallTreatment - see
// that file's own header for the D_h/A_wall/sink derivation, unchanged
// here since neither q_w nor the geometry changed.
//
// ============================================================================
// >>> READ THIS FIRST: every number in this STATUS block and in the first
// >>> three UPDATE blocks below is SUPERSEDED. The S13.4.1/S32.5.5 UPDATE
// >>> block is the current measurement of this file AS SHIPPED; the S37 block
// >>> after it is a separate experiment run on scratchpad COPIES with one
// >>> token added (`PrtModel`, which defaults to `constant` and is absent
// >>> here), so it changes nothing in this file. The blocks in between are
// >>> kept, in order, as the record of how this gate got there.
// STATUS (SPEC-LIT S34 rebuild, RUN and REPORTED - docs/07-fire-solver.md
// S1.1 has the full table): the previous ("mesh b") version of this file was
// a DUCT - real `wallSide.*` walls at only 4 spanwise cells - and collapsed
// to U_b = 0.24 m/s (WF duct twin: 3.51 m/s; laminar solve of the same duct
// mesh: 14.76 m/s). The leading untested lead was the `E` term's
// `grad(grad U)` boundary extrapolation at the duct CORNERS.
//
// THIS rebuild removes the corners. Result: THE VELOCITY COLLAPSE IS FIXED -
// the corner hypothesis is CONFIRMED, not merely plausible. `|U|` converges
// to round-off (2e-12 at 40 000 iterations), U_b = 4.846 m/s (below the
// laminar closed form 34.67 m/s by 7.2x, and below the WF twin's 5.37 m/s),
// U_b/u_tau = 17.35 - INSIDE the 15-17 plane-channel target, closer than the
// WF twin's own 19.23. Measured y+ = 0.00175 (< 1) and 192/400 cells (48%)
// at y+ < 20, both bit-identical at 40 000 AND 150 000 iterations.
//
// BUT a new, third, genuinely different problem: T_b/T_w drift UPWARD at a
// small, LINEAR, UNDAMPED rate for as long as the run is extended (checked
// to 150 000 iterations - the 10 000-iteration increment is 0.377-0.384 K
// throughout, with zero sign of decay). Nu starts inside Dittus-Boelter's
// band at 15 000 iterations (Nu=74.8, +10.0%/+18.4% vs DB/Gnielinski) and
// drifts to OUTSIDE BOTH bands by 150 000 (Nu=89.2, +31%/+41%). Four causes
// ruled out directly: not the turbulence model (kEpsilon on the identical
// mesh drifts the same way), not the grading severity (expansion 20 drifts
// too), not the T-solver's linear-solve tolerance (exact solve reproduces
// the identical trajectory), not open-vs-sealed (heat in == sink out
// exactly by construction, so dp0/dt = 0 either way). Leading explanation:
// q_w and the sink are both T-INDEPENDENT, buoyancy is off (gravity=0), and
// T is Dirichlet nowhere in this domain, so the domain-mean temperature has
// no restoring term - why the COARSE WF mesh nonetheless lands on an exact
// fixed point while this fine mesh does not is not resolved here. Reported,
// not tuned away - see docs/07-fire-solver.md S1.1 for the full table.
// ============================================================================
//
// ============================================================================
// UPDATE (SPEC-LIT S35, the bulk-temperature thermostat): the diagnosis
// above was right about the MECHANISM (no restoring term on the domain
// mean) and wrong only in calling it unresolved - S35.1's thermostat IS
// that restoring term, replacing the fixed `-heaterPower -3.2` sink with a
// `sources[]` thermostat (target 293.15 K, tau 0.02 s, the SAME target as
// `channelPeriodicFluxWF.jsonc`'s own copy - the two legs are now compared
// at the same bulk state for the first time). Rerun, 40 000 iterations:
//
// * THE DRIFT IS GONE. T is bit-identical (T [290.421, 313.879] K, every
//   printed digit) from iteration 5 000 through 39 999 - not merely slower,
//   genuinely stopped, unlike the old undamped ~0.038 K/100-iterations climb.
// * THE REGRESSION SPEC-LIT S35.2 ASKS FOR PASSES: rerun from T0 = 293.15 K
//   and T0 = 400 K converge to the IDENTICAL state - T_mean = 293.574 K,
//   T_b = 292.817 K, U_b = 4.84388 m/s, thermostat power = -3.28977 W -
//   every one of these to the last printed digit, from either start.
// * Energy balance (S35.2's table): thermostat power -3.28977 W against
//   3.2 W of measured wall heat - a 2.8% gap, NOT round-off (unlike the WF
//   leg's 2.8e-7 W). Traced to this mesh's own pressure-solve tolerance
//   floor: `contErr` plateaus at 9.2e-8 (identical from either initial
//   condition - a property of the converged fixed point, not a transient),
//   and tightening `p`'s relTol from 0.01 to 1e-4 DIVERGES the run at
//   iteration 3 317 (NaN) - this graded mesh (`expansion: 200`) needs the
//   looser tolerance to stay stable, and the small residual it leaves feeds
//   into the bounded convection scheme's own div(phi) correction. Reported,
//   not tuned away.
// * THE GATE: Re = 25,834, Nu = 73.40 - Dittus-Boelter +8.1% (inside its
//   own ±20-25% band), Gnielinski +16.3% (OUTSIDE its own ±10% band). The
//   wall-function twin closes on BOTH correlations (see that file's own
//   UPDATE); this leg closes on Dittus-Boelter only. Two-mesh ratio:
//   Nu_lowRe / Nu_WF = 1.125. VERDICT: the gate does NOT close on both legs
//   under SPEC-LIT S32.4's own rule (both correlations, both meshes) - but
//   this is now a SIX-POINT miss on ONE correlation at a converged, stable,
//   reproducible bulk state, not the +31%/+41%-and-still-drifting failure
//   above. What it implicates: with the domain-mean bookkeeping fixed, the
//   remaining gap plausibly reflects Launder-Sharma's own near-wall THERMAL
//   prediction accuracy (SPEC-LIT S33.3 validated its MOMENTUM law of the
//   wall independently; nothing here has yet validated its temperature
//   sublayer the same way) rather than another energy-accounting defect.
//   See `docs/07-fire-solver.md` S1.1 for the full table and the
//   two-initial-temperature trace.
// ============================================================================
//
// SPEC-LIT S29.3: "lowRe... pins the molecular resistance the resolved mesh
// already provides", so the hot walls here are `fixedFluxTemperature` with
// the SAME q - not `thermalWallFunction` - because BcKind::FixedFluxTemperature
// reads `k_eff_wall` off whatever `crate::energy::Energy::update_k_eff`
// computed at that face, and on a `lowRe` wall (`nu_t,w = 0` pinned) that is
// the molecular conductivity alone: no Jayatilleke correction is invoked, or
// needed, because the resolved sublayer supplies its own resistance exactly
// as SPEC-LIT S32.2 asks.
//
// ============================================================================
// UPDATE (SPEC-LIT S32.4/S32.5, WHICH FRICTION FACTOR - comments only; the
// case itself is byte-for-byte the one every number above was measured with):
// the "+16.3%, OUTSIDE Gnielinski's band" verdict above was taken with
// Gnielinski evaluated at Petukhov's SMOOTH-PIPE friction factor
// f = (0.79 ln Re - 1.64)^-2 = 0.02452. Gnielinski is explicitly a function
// of f, and this case is a plane CHANNEL: parallel plates run a higher f
// than a pipe at the same Re_Dh (Jones, ASME J. Fluids Eng. 98 (1976) 173).
// S32.4 now requires every verdict to NAME the f it used, because there are
// two of them and they are different claims:
//
//   * ABSOLUTE PREDICTION, at the pipe f = 0.02452: Nu_Gn = 63.10, measured
//     73.40 is +16.3% - OUTSIDE the +-10% band. THE GATE IS STILL OPEN ON
//     THIS VERDICT. Nothing below changes that.
//   * REYNOLDS ANALOGY, at the f THIS LEG realises, 0.02653 (+8.2% over the
//     pipe correlation): Nu_Gn = 68.72, measured 73.40 is +6.8% - inside the
//     band. The WF twin lands at +6.4% on the same verdict, so BOTH legs
//     pass it, within 0.4 points of each other.
//
// The Reynolds-analogy verdict is the WEAKER claim - it is handed the
// momentum instead of predicting it - and may never be quoted as an
// absolute-prediction pass (S32.4's own rule).
//
// The two legs realise f 22.7% apart at the SAME body force (U_b 4.844 vs
// 5.370 m/s, f ~ 1/U_b^2), and Gnielinski at those two f predicts a two-mesh
// Nu ratio of 1.121 against the measured 1.125 - so most of the 1.125 ratio
// this header attributes to Launder-Sharma's near-wall THERMAL prediction is
// accounted for by a MOMENTUM difference instead. Hypothesis, not finding.
//
// BOTH friction factors above are INFERRED from the steady force balance on
// this file's own `bodyForce` plus the recorded U_b - not measured at the
// wall. S32.5.1 specifies the direct measurement (wall-face viscous
// traction, the `lowRe` form on this leg and the wall-function form on the
// WF twin); `ofgpu-fire` now measures it, prints it next to the body-force
// inference with their disagreement, and prints Gnielinski BOTH ways.
// NEITHER CASE HAS BEEN RERUN WITH IT. See docs/07-fire-solver.md S1.1.
// SUPERSEDED: both cases have since been rerun with it, and the measurement
// retired the Reynolds-analogy verdicts above - see the S32.5.3/S35.3 block
// at the end of this header.
// ============================================================================
//
// ============================================================================
// UPDATE (SPEC-LIT S32.5.3/S35.3 - THE DECISIVE EXPERIMENT, and THIS FILE
// CHANGED): the thermostat above now carries `"weighting": "massFlux"`. Every
// number in the blocks above was measured with the UNIFORM sink and is kept
// as the record; the numbers below are what this file, as it now stands,
// produces.
//
// THE EXPERIMENT S35.3 EXISTS FOR, run on THIS mesh with nothing else
// changed - the same case, 40 000 iterations, twice, with `"weighting"` the
// only token that differs. The uniform run reproduces every recorded number
// above to the last printed digit (T_b = 292.817 K, T_w = 314.087 K,
// U_b = 4.84388 m/s, Nu = 73.4006, power -3.28977 W, y+ 0.00174585,
// 192/400 cells), so this is a controlled comparison:
//
//              uniform      massFlux     change
//   T_w        314.087 K    314.909 K    +0.822 K
//   T_b        292.817 K    292.759 K    -0.058 K
//   T_w - T_b   21.2703 K    22.1503 K   +4.14%
//   Nu          73.4006      70.4707     -3.99%
//   U_b          4.84388      4.8357     -0.17%
//
// S35.3.2's PREDICTION IS CONFIRMED, in sign and in mechanism: the uniform
// sink over-cools the near-wall fluid, so replacing it WIDENS (T_w - T_b) and
// LOWERS Nu. The WF twin moves only -1.41% on the same change, 2.8x less,
// which is the other half of that prediction (the bias lives in the near-wall
// velocity deficit, which this mesh resolves and the WF mesh hides inside a
// wall function). Two-mesh ratio 1.125 -> 1.096: this mechanism accounts for
// 24% of the two-mesh disagreement, measured rather than argued.
//
// THIS FILE AS SHIPPED (massFlux, 40 000 iterations): Re = 25,790,
// Nu = 70.4707, y+ 0.00174, 192/400 cells at y+ < 20, |U| residual 2.8e-12,
// thermostat power -3.30425 W against 3.2 W of wall heat (+3.26%).
//
// VERDICTS (S32.4 - every band statement names its f and carries this leg's
// own energy-balance gap as an uncertainty on Nu):
//   * ABSOLUTE PREDICTION, Gnielinski at the Petukhov smooth-PIPE
//     f = 0.024532: Nu_Gn = 63.021, measured is +11.8% - still OUTSIDE
//     +-10%, but the weighting took it there from +16.3%, and the +-3.26%
//     this leg's energy imbalance puts on Nu is wider than the 1.8 points it
//     misses by. THE GATE DOES NOT CLOSE ON THIS LEG - and for the first
//     time the miss is not decisive either.
//   * Dittus-Boelter 67.8262: +3.9% - inside its own +-20-25% band (it was
//     +8.1%).
//   * REYNOLDS ANALOGY, at the f this leg's WALL now MEASURES, 0.023870:
//     Nu_Gn = 61.1812, measured is +15.2% - OUTSIDE. The "+6.8%, inside" this
//     header used to report was taken at f = 0.02653, INFERRED from the body
//     force; the inference was 11% high. The measured f is also 2.7% BELOW
//     the smooth-pipe correlation, where Jones (1976) says parallel plates
//     should run ABOVE it - under-predicted wall friction is now an open
//     MOMENTUM finding of this gate in its own right.
//
// FORCE BALANCE (S32.5.2's correction, in KINEMATIC units): body force
// (g.e_hat) V = 4.9920e-4 m4/s2 against a wall sink of 4.80296e-4 - -3.787%
// (-3.461% at the uniform default), on a run converged to |U| 2.8e-12. That
// is NOT a convergence gap, and it is NOT this mesh: rerunning THIS case with
// the heat removed (walls zeroGradient, thermostat deleted, everything else
// identical) closes the same balance to -0.00%. The imbalance appears only
// when the energy equation is coupled in, and it appears beside an energy
// imbalance of the same size - two symptoms, one defect, and S32.5.3 names a
// mechanism for it (S3.1's bounded convection correction, whose domain
// integral is zero only when div(phi) is) that has not yet been tested.
//
// See docs/07-fire-solver.md S1.1's last subsection for the full account.
// ============================================================================
//
// ============================================================================
// UPDATE (SPEC-LIT S13.4.1/S32.5.5 - THE GATE RERUN ON THE SETTINGS THIS FILE
// ACTUALLY ASKS FOR; comments only, the case itself is unchanged): every
// number in every block above was produced by a driver that read NONE of this
// file's `numerics` block - it ran `bounded Gauss upwind` on the momentum
// equation where this file asks for `Gauss linearUpwind grad(U)` with no
// `bounded` prefix. Fixed (S13.4.1), and both legs rerun.
//
// CONTROL: setting `div(phi,U)` back to `bounded Gauss upwind` by hand
// reproduces the record above exactly - T_b = 292.759 K, T_w = 314.909 K,
// U_b = 4.83570 m/s, Nu = 70.4709, thermostat power -3.30423 W, drag balance
// -3.787%.
//
// THIS FILE AS SHIPPED, at the settings it names (40 000 iterations,
// |U| residual 4.1e-12):
//   T_b = 292.800 K | T_w = 314.186 K | dT = 21.3862 K | U_b = 4.92909 m/s
//   Re = 26,288 | Nu = 72.9988 (was 70.4707: +3.59%)
//   worst y+ 0.00185363 | 192/400 cells at y+ < 20 | contErr 1.10e-7
//   thermostat power -3.29963 W against 3.2 W of wall heat, a +3.11% gap
//   f MEASURED = 0.023936 (viscous - the discrete sink on a lowRe wall)
//   kinematic force balance -0.000% (WAS -3.787%)
//
// TWO RESULTS, pointing opposite ways:
//
// 1. THE DRAG IMBALANCE IS CLOSED, and the mechanism S32.5.3 named as an
//    untested suspect is CONFIRMED by isolation. Four runs of this case
//    differing only in `div(phi,U)`:
//      bounded Gauss upwind              Nu 70.4709  drag -3.787%  energy +3.257%
//      bounded Gauss linearUpwind grad(U) Nu 70.5193  drag -3.788%  energy +3.255%
//      Gauss upwind                      Nu 72.9508  drag +0.000%  energy +3.116%
//      Gauss linearUpwind grad(U)        Nu 72.9988  drag -0.000%  energy +3.114%
//    contErr is 1.101e-7 in all four. So it was never the scheme's ORDER
//    (worth +0.07% of Nu) and never contErr (unchanged): it was the `bounded`
//    token, which in a low-Mach solver subtracts a PRESCRIBED dilatation
//    (S25.1) from the momentum equation as if it were an error. SPEC-LIT S3.1
//    now forbids defaulting a momentum equation to the bounded form.
//    The ENERGY imbalance moved 0.14 points on that same change, so
//    S32.5.3's "one defect with two symptoms" is refuted: the momentum
//    symptom is gone and the energy symptom is not.
//
// 2. THE NUSSELT GATE MOVED FURTHER OUT, and is now decisively open.
//    * ABSOLUTE PREDICTION, Gnielinski at the Petukhov pipe f = 0.024416:
//      Nu_Gn = 63.9587, measured is +14.1% (was +11.8%) - OUTSIDE +-10% by
//      4.1 points, MORE than this leg's own +-3.11% energy-balance
//      uncertainty is wide. S32.4's UNDECIDED clause no longer applies.
//    * Dittus-Boelter 68.8722, +6.0% - inside its own band.
//    * REYNOLDS ANALOGY at the measured f: Nu_Gn = 62.5988, +16.6% - OUTSIDE.
//
// WHAT IS LEFT. This leg's measured f is only -2.0% of the Petukhov pipe f
// and its drag balance closes exactly, so it now transports very nearly the
// right MOMENTUM and 14% too much HEAT - a thermal finding with nothing left
// on the momentum side to carry it. The leading named candidate is the
// case-wide constant Prt = 0.85 reaching a first cell at y+ = 0.0019, where
// Kays (ASME J. Heat Transfer 116 (1994) 284) puts Prt at 1.5-1.9; a
// hypothesis with a mechanism and the right sign, measured by nothing yet.
//
// See docs/07-fire-solver.md S1.1's last subsection for the full account.
// ============================================================================
//
// ============================================================================
// UPDATE (SPEC-LIT S37 - THE NAMED CANDIDATE, MEASURED; comments only, THIS
// FILE IS UNCHANGED): the block above ends by naming the case-wide constant
// Prt = 0.85 as the one remaining candidate for this leg's +14.1% Gnielinski
// miss - a hypothesis with a mechanism and the right sign and no measurement.
// S37 implements Kays-Crawford's variable Prt(Pe_t) (Kays, ASME J. Heat
// Transfer 116 (1994) 284; Kays & Crawford 4th ed. ch. 13) as a SELECTABLE
// model, `physics.fluid.PrtModel`, DEFAULTING TO `constant` precisely so this
// file does not move and every number above stays reproducible.
//
// THE EXPERIMENT: this case and its WF twin, 40 000 iterations each, run
// twice on scratchpad copies with `"PrtModel": "KaysCrawford"` added and
// NOTHING else changed. The `constant` runs reproduce every number above to
// the last printed digit.
//
//   THIS MESH        constant        KaysCrawford     change
//   T_w              314.186 K       315.692 K
//   T_b              292.800 K       292.748 K
//   T_w - T_b         21.3862 K       22.9439 K       +7.28%
//   Nu                72.9988         68.0305         -6.81%
//   U_b                4.92909 m/s     4.92984 m/s    +0.015%  (a THERMAL model)
//   Prt in use         0.85            [0.8701, 1.7000], volume-mean 0.9042,
//                                      wall-adjacent cells EXACTLY 1.7000
//   energy balance    +3.11%          +3.35%
//   drag balance      -0.000%         +0.000%
//
// The wall-adjacent 1.7000 is S37.2's derived Pe_t -> 0 limit, reached
// because LaunderSharmaKE pins nu_t/nu to 3.9e-7 there. The WF twin's Prt
// never leaves [0.8748, 0.8917] - six cells, the nearest at y+ 58 - and its
// Nu moves only -1.45%, 4.7x less. That asymmetry is the mechanism, measured.
//
// VERDICT: at the shipped default this file's verdict above is UNCHANGED
// (+14.1%, OUTSIDE Gnielinski's +-10%). At `KaysCrawford` it is +6.4%,
// INSIDE - and inside across the whole of this leg's own +-3.35%
// energy-balance uncertainty ([+2.8%, +9.9%]) - with Dittus-Boelter at -1.2%
// and the Reynolds-analogy verdict at +9.3%. NOTHING WAS TUNED: C = 0.3 and
// Pr_t_inf = 0.85 are the published constants and Pr_t_inf is this file's own
// `Prt`. The predicted fall was 10-14%; the measured fall is 6.8%, about
// half, and that is reported rather than rounded.
//
// See docs/07-fire-solver.md S1.1's last subsection for the four runs, the
// two-mesh consequence, and what this does NOT establish.
// ============================================================================
{
  "$schema": "https://meteor-cfd.msimul.com/schema/case-1.json",
  "name": "channelPeriodicFluxLowRe",

  "mesh": {
    "kind": "cartesian",
    "bounds": { "min": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0], "max": [0.08, 0.04, 0.04] },
    "cells":  [8, 50, 1],

    "boundaries": {
      "xmin": "streamwiseMin", "xmax": "streamwiseMax",
      "ymin": "wallHot0", "ymax": "wallHot1",
      "zmin": "back", "zmax": "front",
    },

    "cyclic": [ { "a": "streamwiseMin", "b": "streamwiseMax", "transform": "translate" } ],

    "grading": { "y": { "expansion": 200.0, "twoSided": true } },
  },

  "physics": {
    "gravity": [0, 0, 0],
    "fluid": { "nu": 1.5e-5, "Pr": 0.71, "Prt": 0.85, "TRef": 293.15 },
    "buoyancy": "densityRatio",
  },

  "turbulence": {
    "kind": "RAS",
    "model": "LaunderSharmaKE",
    "wallFunctions": { "kappa": 0.41, "E": 9.8 },
    "wallTreatment": "lowRe",
  },

  "sources": [
    { "type": "momentumSource", "field": "U", "bodyForce": [3.9, 0, 0] },
    // SPEC-LIT S35.1: replaces the fixed -heaterPower -3.2 compensation
    // with the bulk-temperature thermostat - IDENTICAL target to
    // `channelPeriodicFluxWF.jsonc`'s own copy (293.15 K, this case's own
    // `initial.T`/`TRef`) and the same tau, so the two legs are compared
    // at the SAME bulk state for the first time (S35's own point - see
    // that file's own comment for the full derivation). This is also the
    // fix for the header's own "third, genuinely different problem" above:
    // T_b/T_w drifting upward without decay was exactly the symptom of the
    // free additive constant S35 diagnoses; a domain-mean thermostat is
    // what removes it.
    { "type": "thermostat", "target": 293.15, "tau": 0.02, "weighting": "massFlux" },
  ],

  "patches": [
    {
      // SPEC-LIT S34.1: the 2-D constraint, not a boundary condition - no
      // per-field BC may be named alongside it (checked and refused). This
      // is what replaces the old duct's `wallSide.*` real walls: there is
      // no side-wall boundary layer left to interact with the hot-wall one
      // at a corner, which is the point of this rebuild.
      "match": "(back|front)",
      "kind": "empty",
    },
    {
      // Same q_w as `channelPeriodicFluxWF.jsonc` - see that file's own
      // header and this file's own for why `fixedFluxTemperature` (not
      // `thermalWallFunction`) is correct on a `lowRe` wall. The
      // `sources[]` thermostat above is what now balances it (S35.1).
      "match": "wallHot.*",
      "kind": "wall",
      "U": { "type": "fixedValue", "value": [0, 0, 0] },
      "p": { "type": "zeroGradient" },
      "T": { "type": "fixedFluxTemperature", "q": 500.0 },
    },
    {
      "match": ".*",
      "kind": "wall",
      "U": { "type": "fixedValue", "value": [0, 0, 0] },
      "p": { "type": "zeroGradient" },
      "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" },
    },
  ],

  "initial": {
    "U": [3.0, 0, 0],
    "T": 293.15,
    "p": 0.0,
    "k": 0.03,
    "epsilon": 0.3,
    "nut": 0,
  },

  "numerics": {
    "algorithm": { "kind": "SIMPLE" },
    "ddt": "steadyState",
    "div": {
      "default": "Gauss upwind",
      "div(phi,U)": "Gauss linearUpwind grad(U)",
      "div(phi,T)": "bounded Gauss upwind",
      "div(phi,k)": "bounded Gauss upwind",
      "div(phi,epsilon)": "bounded Gauss upwind",
    },
    "grad": "Gauss linear",
    "laplacian": { "snGrad": "corrected", "nonOrthogonalCorrectors": 0 },
    "relaxation": { "U": 0.5, "p": 0.3, "T": 0.5, "k": 0.5, "epsilon": 0.5 },
    "solvers": [
      { "match": "p",       "solver": "PBiCGStab", "preconditioner": "DIC",      "tolerance": 1e-9, "relTol": 0.01, "maxIter": 1000 },
      { "match": "U",       "solver": "PBiCGStab", "preconditioner": "diagonal", "tolerance": 1e-9, "relTol": 0.1,  "maxIter": 200 },
      { "match": "T",       "solver": "PBiCGStab", "preconditioner": "diagonal", "tolerance": 1e-9, "relTol": 0.01, "maxIter": 200 },
      { "match": "k",       "solver": "PBiCGStab", "preconditioner": "diagonal", "tolerance": 1e-9, "relTol": 0.01, "maxIter": 200 },
      { "match": "epsilon", "solver": "PBiCGStab", "preconditioner": "diagonal", "tolerance": 1e-9, "relTol": 0.01, "maxIter": 200 },
    ],
  },

  "run": {
    "endTime": 1.0,
